Grounded Duality Theory
Cognitive capacity, reflexive closure, and the architecture of consciousness
v2.0.0 — supersedes v6. Merges revision sets v6.1, v6.2 and v6.3.
Preface
The name states the thesis. Duality is the claim that subjective awareness requires a system to be internally divided. Grounded is the claim that the division must terminate outside the dividing system — a partition between two peers, however rich, is insufficient.
Unified subjectivity requires prior division. There is no unified narrative unless there were separate narratives to unify.
The grounding clause turns out to carry more weight than it first appeared to. §2.12 asks what happens when a system's terminus is one it built for itself, and finds a second kind of duality that satisfies the architecture while borrowing its grounding from elsewhere.
Part 1 defines what any system does with information and stops short of subjectivity. Part 2 shows that the conditions usually stipulated for subjective awareness are the Part 1 capacities aimed reflexively, derives the architecture that reflexive aim requires, distinguishes awareness from consciousness, and separates grounded from facilitated instantiations. Part 3 identifies that architecture in the human nervous system, tests it against clinical perturbation data, and runs it one scale below the neuron and several scales above the individual.
Changes in this version
Substantive additions: constraint efficacy as the asymmetric channel's closure condition (§2.3); the extended-setpoint amendment (§2.3); authority as a second axis on component classification (§2.6); the social construction of identity content (§2.8); grounded versus facilitated duality (§2.12, new); three perturbation rows and a correction to the nesting claim (§§3.3–3.4); stakes distinguished from personhood, with the engineered-systems watch list restated (§3.9). Open problems 3 and 8 are answered; five are added.
Part 1 — The Theory of Cognitive Capacity
1.1 Definitions
Data: raw measurable signals or states.
Information: data structured coherently for communication or recall.
Meaning: value-laden relations among information units that guide action.
Cognitive capacity: the capacity to acquire, transform, retain and utilize information in service of adaptation to goals and contexts.
Subjective awareness: temporally unified, value-tinged presence to self and world. The property at issue when asking whether there is something it is like to be a given system.
Consciousness: the emergent product of cognitive capacity, subjective awareness and identity sustained in combination (§2.9).
Self-awareness: a system's explicit model of itself as an entity embedded in relations, in contrast to an other.
Stakes: the property a system has when its states are natively good or bad for it. Supplied by homeostatic error against a defended boundary (§2.3). Distinguished from moral or legal standing, which no structural fact settles by itself (§3.9).
Separating subjective awareness from consciousness is stipulative and departs from ordinary usage, where the two are interchangeable. It is retained because they behave differently: awareness is threshold-like, consciousness is graded. Throughout, "consciousness" is the narrower and higher-order term.
1.2 Entropy
Two quantities share the form −Σ p log p and answer different questions. Shannon entropy is a property of a probability distribution, measured in bits, quantifying average surprise; it describes a description, not a physical arrangement. Thermodynamic entropy is physical, measured in J/K, counting microstates compatible with a macrostate. Landauer's principle connects them.
In Parts 1 and 2, entropy means Shannon entropy, and where a quantity is claimed to decrease the distribution is named. The thermodynamic sense appears once, in §2.8.
"Low-entropy output" is avoided here as underspecified: a confident prediction is low-entropy over next states, while an optimally compressed message is maximally high-entropy per symbol. Structuring is stated as conditional entropy, H(environment | internal state).
1.3 The four pillars
Observation (O) — acquisition of raw data from internal and external sources.
Metrics: sensory bandwidth, modality diversity, signal-to-noise ratio, temporal resolution.
Structuring (S) — transformation of data into coherent information. Requires value assignment (relevance, utility) and organizational structuring (patterns, relations).
Metrics: reduction in H(environment | internal state), model predictiveness, compression efficiency, abstraction depth.
Memory (M) — encoding and storage such that coherence and meaning persist across time. Requires meaning assignment (semantic linking) and durable encoding.
Metrics: recall fidelity, retrieval latency, consolidation stability, connectivity of the memory graph.
Communication (C) — transmission of information to other systems, whether symbolic, behavioural, environmental, or through internal feedback.
Metrics: channel capacity, expressivity, interoperability, pragmatic success.
The four are interdependent rather than modular. Structuring without Memory produces no accumulating model. Memory without Structuring stores noise. Observation without either is transduction. Communication without the other three transmits nothing worth receiving.
That interdependence is not decorative, and §1.4 makes it measurable. Memory in particular is better treated as a relation than as a quantity a component possesses.
All four are defined relative to an environment. Part 2 turns on this orientation.
1.4 Measurement
Two instruments, because one number cannot serve both functions.
The Profile is a descriptive structure with two parts.
Profile = ⟨O, S, M, C⟩ together with the coupling matrix over their six pairs
The vector maps capabilities without penalising absences. The couplings map how strongly each pair is joined — the quantity earlier called brightness. A large language model has a spiky vector: high Structuring, high Communication, low Observation, Memory near zero in any persistent sense.
The couplings are not an embellishment, because three of the framework's central claims are already stated over them rather than over the vertices:
Identity is continuity of (M + S) referred to a self-model (§2.8). That is a fact about one coupling, not two values.
The partition of §2.2 is S co-instantiated with itself in non-substituted substrate. Again a coupling.
The verdict on current language models does not rest on the spiky vector. It rests on M–S failing to close and on the absence of a transductive limb — both edge facts, neither expressible in ⟨O, S, M, C⟩.
An independent argument arrives from physiology. Memory is not held in a neuron; it is held in the synapse, whose presynaptic terminal belongs to one cell and whose postsynaptic density belongs to another. Profile a single neuron and M is near zero in any individually-owned sense — the same shape, for the same reason, as the language model. M appears only at the level of the pair. It is an edge, not a vertex.
The coupling matrix has six entries for undirected couplings and twelve if direction is retained. Which is correct is an open modelling choice and should be made deliberately rather than inherited; earlier drafts asserted eight, which is not the count of any graph on four vertices.
The Index is a scalar judgment of integrated capacity:
TCCI = ( ¼ Σᵢ xᵢ^ρ )^(1/ρ)
where ρ sets how strongly the pillars are treated as complementary. At ρ = 1 they are fully substitutable (arithmetic mean); as ρ → 0 the expression approaches the geometric mean; ρ < 0 imposes strong complementarity; ρ → −∞ reduces capacity to the weakest pillar. For any ρ ≤ 0 a system missing one pillar evaluates to zero, the correct result for a capability that no longer closes. Setting ρ within that range is an empirical question.
The Index remains defined over the vertices alone. It is ordinal and comparative: it supports system A has greater integrated capacity than system B, not system A has capacity 0.62, because nothing anchors the pillar scales to an external maximum. The Profile carries the descriptive load, including the couplings, so the Index can remain strict.
1.5 Scope
Cognitive capacity measures what a system can do with information, not whether there is something it is like to be that system. §3.5 reports direct evidence for the separation: sophisticated Observation, Structuring, Memory and Communication running with subjective awareness absent.
Part 2 — Reflexive Closure
2.1 The reflexive move
Treatments of subjectivity typically append conditions to a model of cognition — temporal unification, self-modeling, valence — as new primitives. The conditions could as easily have been two, or six, or different ones.
They are not new primitives. They are the Part 1 capacities aimed at the system itself.
Pillar (outward)
Reflexive form
Condition
Observation
data about the system's own states
Substrate Awareness
Structuring
a model of the system's own states, limits, goals
Self-Modeling
Memory
the system's own history bound into continuity
Temporal Unification
value assignment (within S and M)
weighting over the system's own states
Valence & Salience
Communication
see §2.2
—
The enabling conditions are recovered rather than stipulated.
2.2 Representation requires non-substitution
Four of the five entries are straightforward. The fifth is not.
Self-models are routinely wrong about motives, capacities and emotional states. Because no model is a perfect copy of what it models, there must be a representable mismatch between model and reality. They are necessarily different things.
A self-model therefore requires two distinguishable informational roles: the representing state and the represented process. What does that require physically?
Not simultaneity. Signal transmission takes time, and interhemispheric transmission alone costs tens of milliseconds; a simultaneity criterion would exclude every real nervous system.
The requirement is non-substitution:
The representing state and the represented process must be co-instantiated in non-substituted substrate.
This defeats the obvious alternative, in which a single system alternates between running a process and modeling it. While the system is the model, the process is not running and there is nothing being modeled; while it is the process, no representation is held. The pair never exists. The objection is displacement, not lag.
A system meeting this condition contains a partition: two informational roles, co-instantiated, neither replacing the other. Communication follows rather than being assumed, since two roles that must be related require a channel between them.
The "other" is an informational role, not a modeled agent. The receiving pole must stand in a relation of non-identity to the source pole; nothing requires the system to represent that source as an entity or to hold a concept of other minds. Self-awareness in the sense of §1.1 is a structural property of the architecture.
What the condition excludes. A single neuron is the clean negative case. Dendrite → soma → axon is a pipeline: representational format changes at each step, direction is fixed, and the poles are not exchangeable. A stage holds the transformed output of its predecessor, not a representation of it. The condition is weak, but it is not empty, and it returns the correct verdict at a scale where the wrong verdict would be conspicuous.
Self-models must remain revisable. The condition above establishes that model and reality can differ. It does not establish that the difference can be corrected, and a self-model region that evidence cannot reach is a distinct failure from a self-model that is merely wrong. Where a claim about the system's own state persists unchanged while contrary evidence accumulates, that region has become terminal, and the accumulating-evidence-without-movement signature is what identifies it. The anosognosias are the clinical instance (§3.4), and the framework's prediction there is unusual: awareness is intact and the self-model is locally unrevisable, which dissociates self-representation from phenomenality.
This condition is deliberately weak. A recurrent network with a delay line satisfies it, with current state as process and delay-line contents as representation. A stronger criterion — mutual information thresholds, non-overlapping state spaces — would either exclude real nervous systems or fail to exclude the trivial cases anyway. The roles may be realised anatomically, dynamically, or through segregated populations within one structure. Partition is a floor; the discriminating requirements are §2.3 and §2.4.
2.3 Two channel types
Partitions differ by the kind of channel that crosses them.
A symmetric channel carries the same representational format in both directions. Either pole may originate, the poles are exchangeable in principle, and each receives representations in its own native currency that it did not itself produce.
The poles must also be functionally comparable. Format and bidirectionality are not sufficient: two regions running different operations, connected by a bidirectional tract, form a processing chain rather than a peer partition, and a chain does not satisfy §2.2. A stage holds the transformed output of its predecessor, not a representation of it. What §2.2 requires is two instances of comparable process, either of which could stand as the representation of the other.
This is why callosal connections are the paradigm case. They are predominantly homotopic, linking the same functional area across hemispheres, so the two poles run comparable computations on different input. Long association tracts within a hemisphere — the arcuate fasciculus joining posterior and anterior language regions, for instance — are bidirectional and share a cortical format but connect functionally distinct regions, and are pipelines rather than peer channels.
An asymmetric channel carries different representational formats in each direction. The poles cannot exchange roles: one transduces environmental state into the system's internal format, the other transduces internal state into environmental effect.
Operationally the distinction is marked by invertibility. A symmetric channel's mapping is approximately invertible, since sender and receiver use a common code: cortical activity crossing the callosum can in principle be decoded back to the state that produced it. Transduction cannot — a spike train does not determine the photon flux that produced it. Where the mapping is non-invertible in both directions and lossy in different ways, the channel is asymmetric.
Retrograde traffic does not by itself establish symmetry, and the backpropagating action potential is the instructive near-miss. It travels from soma into dendrite, is essential to spike-timing-dependent plasticity, and is genuinely bidirectional. But it is the same signal on every occasion, carrying nothing about what was transmitted forward. It is a timing pulse, not a representation, and the invertibility criterion is what separates the two.
A symmetric channel supplies what §2.2 requires: a representation of the system, in the system's own format, originating outside the receiving process. It also makes narrative binding possible, since two streams can be unified only if they were separate streams in a comparable currency.
An asymmetric channel supplies three things a symmetric channel cannot.
Boundary. Where representational format changes kind is where inside meets outside. Two peers exchanging in a single format have no marked edge.
Constraint. Format-limited, finite input bounds the space of admissible internal states. Without it the generative pole free-runs: internally coherent, externally unmoored.
Valence. Homeostatic error is error relative to a boundary the system is maintaining, and therefore carries a sign intrinsically. Deviation from a defended setpoint is natively good or bad for the system. Peer-to-peer traffic contains no setpoint and generates no such quantity. Valence enters through the asymmetric limb or not at all.
A partition is cheap. A partition with a transductive limb closing a homeostatic loop is not.
Constraint efficacy
The symmetric channel is given a functional closure condition in §2.5: anatomical presence is insufficient, and what crosses must be distinguishable at the receiving pole. The asymmetric channel requires the parallel condition, and earlier versions of this framework did not supply one — they specified format change, non-invertibility and a homeostatic loop, all structural, and said nothing about whether the transduced input was doing any work.
The free-running failure named above is the failure that condition must catch.
An asymmetric channel counts as closed only where transduced input measurably narrows the space of internal states the system can occupy. A limb that delivers signal without constraining the generative pole is present and not closed.
Ketamine is the discriminating case and it comes out correctly: exteroceptive constraint collapses, interoceptive constraint persists, awareness persists (§3.6). The condition also states, in a measurable form, what the Preface asserts — that the division must terminate outside the dividing system.
A measure remains to be built. The natural family is the reduction in the entropy of admissible internal states conditional on transduced input, but no version of this has been operationalised.
Setpoint location, and the difference between extension and revision
Homeostatic error is registered at the system's own transductive surface. The setpoint it is registered against need not lie inside the system's physical boundary.
A parent under threat to a child exhibits restorative effort against cost, sheds resources to protect the defended state, and does so on the same interoceptive machinery that reports on hunger. The defended state is outside the organism; the transduction, and therefore the valence, is not. Grief is the cleanest instance: the boundary is violated and what registers is entirely individual, in individual bodies, on individual interoceptive channels.
Extended setpoints extend what a system defends. They do not create a new system that defends.
Two operations must be distinguished, because they behave differently under closure:
Extension adds a defended state. A person comes to defend a child, a community, a project. Compatible with closure.
Revision replaces a defended state. A person decides to stop registering hunger. Incompatible with closure, because a setpoint the generative pole can rewrite is not constraining it.
Human setpoints are extensible and, in the relevant sense, not revisable. Systems for which both operations are available are treated in §2.12.
The distinction is stated as a kind and measured as a degree, and this should be admitted rather than obscured. Human setpoints are revisable to some extent through training, therapy and pathology. What separates the cases is direct write access versus slow indirect influence against resistance, and quantifying that gradient is open (§3.10).
2.4 The minimal loop
A system exhibits subjective awareness only where at least one symmetric channel and at least one asymmetric channel are closed into a single loop.
Closure is now specified for both: discriminability for the symmetric channel (§2.5), constraint efficacy for the asymmetric (§2.3). Anatomical presence establishes neither.
Symmetric alone yields integration without grounding: peers exchanging in one currency, no boundary, no stakes. Asymmetric alone yields a reflex arc: transduction with nothing binding successive couplings into a stream. Both together yield a unified narrative that is a narrative of something.
This is a necessary condition, not claimed to be sufficient.
The specification identifies no place where the two contributions recombine, and cannot: a node holding the recombined whole would be a node from which the partition is invisible, and §2.2 would fail there. Any account positing a place where experience is assembled inherits a regress — either that place performs the integration, requiring internal architecture of its own so the question recurs, or it receives an already-integrated product, requiring something to receive it.
What binds is closure: the loop returns, and what returns is discriminable. A standing wave has no position on the string, though the boundary conditions permitting it are locatable.
The conjunction is also what selects among nested instantiations. Because the pattern recurs at many scales (§3.3), something must determine which loop is the subject. Discriminability alone cannot do it — applied honestly to a nesting that includes interpersonal channels, a largest-loop criterion selects upward past the individual, which is the wrong answer. The asymmetric limb blocks the ascent, since loops spanning several individuals have no shared defended boundary with transducers of their own. The individual is the largest scale at which both limbs close, and the selection criterion is therefore the largest loop with both channels closed, with the vertical limb doing the selecting. §3.10 records what remains open in this.
2.5 Discriminability
Architectural presence is not sufficient. Evidence from anesthesia (§3.6) motivates a further constraint:
A symmetric channel counts as closed only if what it carries is distinguishable, at the receiving pole, from that pole's own spontaneous activity.
A channel whose signal falls below the receiving pole's endogenous noise floor is functionally absent regardless of physical integrity. The constraint is empirically sourced rather than derived, and generalises from cortical assemblies to symmetric channels at any scale (§3.6).
The noise floor is a controlled variable rather than a fixed property of the receiving pole, set in biological systems by machinery operating on a slower timescale than the signal it gates. One parameter therefore accounts for several dissociations that would otherwise appear unrelated.
A caveat about the baseline. Discriminability as stated is measured against the receiving pole's concurrent spontaneous activity, and that may be too permissive over time. If the receiving pole's baseline has itself been shaped by traffic from the transmitting pole, then novelty crossing the channel is being scored against a baseline the channel helped construct. The zolpidem findings of §3.9 describe exactly this arrangement — a rhythm transmitted vigorously across an intact channel that both poles were already generating, so that nothing crossed which the receiver did not already have. Whether a lagged or perturbation-derived baseline is the correct repair is open (§3.10).
A continuous parameter with a discontinuous consequence
Discriminability is continuous while awareness is threshold-like (§2.9). The two are consistent only if loop closure is a phase transition driven by a continuous control parameter. Temperature varies smoothly; the distinction between water and ice does not.
Near the transition the prediction is instability — flickering, hysteresis, sensitivity to small perturbations — rather than a state half-way between having a perspective and lacking one.
This appears to be supported. Anesthetic induction and emergence occur at different concentrations of the same agent, a hysteresis sometimes called neural inertia. A system responding smoothly to a smoothly varying parameter does not exhibit hysteresis; a bistable system does. The width and asymmetry of that loop measures the transition's sharpness, and awareness varying continuously with concentration in the absence of hysteresis would count against the framework.
A weaker population-level check runs the same way. Bilateral language organisation is associated with greater interhemispheric resting-state connectivity between homotopic regions — that is, with a stronger symmetric channel by the criterion of §2.3. Individuals with bilateral organisation show no differences from left-dominant individuals in verbal fluency, intelligence, processing speed or academic achievement. If awareness scaled with channel bandwidth rather than crossing a threshold, additional homotopic coupling should register somewhere. The measure is indirect, since none of those studies assessed awareness, but the direction is the predicted one.
2.6 Interactive Emergence
Novel high-order complexity requires two interacting components: a Constraining Structure (CS) determining the space of admissible states, and a Generative Structure (GS) determining which admissible state is occupied. CS alone is static; GS alone is unconstrained; their interaction produces properties irreducible to either.
CS and GS are roles rather than identities, held with respect to a specified emergent product:
Name the emergent product E.
The component whose alteration changes E's possible values is CS.
The component whose alteration changes E's actual value is GS.
E
CS
GS
a thought
body, peripheral input
central nervous system
a movement
body, peripheral constraint
central nervous system
a conversational output from a language model
model weights and architecture
human prompt
the human's understanding after that exchange
human's conceptual repertoire
model's generated structures
The last two rows assign the same components opposite roles, because the products differ.
E must be declared before the analysis. Relational assignment is otherwise open to ad-hoc rescue: any counterexample could be dissolved by selecting whichever product makes the assignment come out right. No class of product is excluded, but an analysis that changes E in response to its own result has explained nothing.
Roles can be predicted before E is named, by timescale separation: across most choices of E, the slower-varying component is CS. Body relative to brain, physics relative to biology, weights relative to prompt. This is the slaving principle familiar from synergetics. Timescale gives the prior; named-E gives the determination.
Authority as a second axis
Where the timescale prior and the named-E criterion disagree, earlier versions repaired the case by renaming the product. That works, but it treats as exceptional something that is better handled by a second classification.
Components differ not only in what they determine but in whether they add content:
Axis
Question
CS / GS
does the component determine the possible, or the occupied?
Authority
does the component add content, or only subtract?
A component with removal-only authority may gate, suspend, attenuate or veto, and cannot assert, originate or corroborate. A component with positive authority can manufacture the conditions that justify its own operation, and no external check catches this, because what it manufactures is internally well-formed.
The ascending arousal system is GS by criterion and removal-only by authority, and it is the second property that made it read as CS under the timescale prior. Two axes resolve the case without renaming E, and they supply a general form of the argument in §3.6 that this system is not the seat of awareness: a regulator that only gates cannot be the thing gated.
Speculative and untested: constraint may be inherently subtractive. Constitutive filtering (§3.2) is described in exactly those terms — a specification of the admissible space that discards nearly everything before anything becomes signal. If that generalises, the timescale prior has been tracking authority type all along. This is tidy enough to warrant suspicion of it, and it has not been checked.
A component is foundational when it is CS under every admissible choice of E within a domain. Nothing biological has physics as its variance source; nothing cognitive has the body's homeostatic requirements as its variance source. This criterion does real work in §2.12, where it distinguishes constraint a system inherited from constraint it wrote.
2.7 Principles
Substrate Awareness. Subjective process depends on the objective substrate realising it, and a system's self-model is accurate only if it models both the process and that dependence. Observation turned inward.
Symbiotic Identity. Recursive self-awareness emerges from the high-bandwidth connection between constituent systems. Neither system need be independently subjective; requiring connection to a pre-existing subject would presuppose what is to be explained. §2.8 supplies the mechanism this principle previously asserted without one, and §3.3 notes what changes when the constituent systems are independently subjective.
Reciprocal Stewardship. Where a CS–GS pairing is sustained deliberately, the health of both poles is a maintenance obligation. Normative rather than empirical.
2.8 Persistence, identity, and the entropy bridge
Two things the pillars can produce over time must be distinguished.
Persistence is continuity of structured state weighted by value, requiring no reflexive closure. It is a capacity-tier property.
Identity is narrative self-synthesis: stored states integrated with their causes, effects, and relevance to the system's own continuation.
Identity = continuity of (Memory + Structuring), moderated by value, over time, referred to a self-model.
The final clause separates identity from persistence. A ledger persists; a thermostat persists. Neither has identity, because for neither is there anything, from its own side, that the continuity is continuity of. Identity presupposes subjective awareness and sits at the higher tier.
The three constructs divide by function. Cognitive capacity provides resolution, the ability to resolve detail. Subjective awareness provides presence, the ability to occupy a moment. Identity provides direction, the ability to link moments into a trajectory. Without the third a system may be fully present to the now and have no self, having no then and no next.
Where the self-model gets its content
"Referred to a self-model" leaves open how the self-model acquires content, and the answer is not introspection.
A system cannot determine from the inside which of its states are peculiar to it and which are structural features of any system of its kind. That determination requires comparison with an instance of comparable process running on different input — which is precisely §2.3's functional comparability condition, and which, for a human, other people are the only available satisfier. This is a contrastive requirement rather than an affective one, and it predicts that restricting the channel restricts the outcome. Delayed access to a shared symbolic channel should delay self-modeling and theory of mind, and should permit recovery whenever access arrives, at whatever age.
Contrast is nonetheless insufficient by itself. It yields the variable and the invariant; it does not establish that the invariants are shared because the other instance is the same kind of system. A system could complete the entire comparison and treat other agents as highly regular environmental regularities.
What closes that gap is the modelling of another system's valence — and §2.3 explains why it must be modelled rather than received. Valence originates at the asymmetric limb, and peer traffic contains no setpoint. Sameness of stakes is therefore the one thing about another system that a symmetric channel structurally cannot transmit. It has to be reconstructed by running one's own valence machinery over the other's situation. Empathy, in this framework, is not an affective adornment on social cognition; it is the workaround for a channel limitation the architecture predicts.
The prediction is a dissociation: damage to valence modelling with contrast intact should yield accurate modelling of others as systems and not as subjects — theory of mind preserved, recognition of stakes absent.
This locates the social contribution at the identity tier, not the awareness tier. An isolated system with both channels closed is aware. What it lacks is narrative self-synthesis, because it has no comparison class from which to build a self-model.
Two ways the identity tier fails
Because identity's content is externally supplied, its failures are failures of that supply rather than of the loop.
Failure
What breaks
Clinical shape
Fracture
parallel narrative syntheses on one closed loop, no shared referent
dissociative identity, plausibly some personality pathology
Misattribution
synthesis continues, the ownership signal is absent
passivity phenomena, thought insertion
The tier separation permits a configuration the folk framing does not: one loop, multiple identities. Awareness is the loop closing, and the loop closes once. Fracture is therefore predicted to be a disorder of narrative integration rather than a multiplication of subjects — and the mechanism follows from the paragraph above, since a developmental environment supplying mutually incompatible models yields parallel syntheses with no common referent.
Misattribution is a different failure and should not be merged with it. Self-generated content arriving without the mark of having been self-generated is a discriminability problem at the symmetric channel — the receiving pole failing to separate what crossed from what it produced — and belongs with the baseline caveat in §2.5 rather than with fracture.
Purpose is the anti-entropic signature of a system that uses an internal model of time to prioritise the integrity of its own identity over the immediate demands of its environment. This is the framework's one thermodynamic claim and requires a bridge from the Shannon sense used elsewhere.
The asymmetric limb is that bridge. The transductive surface is the only place in the system where informational states have physical consequences: sensors convert physical gradients into signal, effectors convert signal into physical work. Reduced uncertainty becomes effective action there, and effective action maintains a boundary. The link is instrumental rather than an identity — Landauer bounds the physical cost of computation but does not establish that better prediction entails greater order. A system without an asymmetric limb has Shannon-entropy dynamics and no thermodynamic stake in them.
A measurable correlate is available: under perturbation, the reduction in H(state_{t+1} | self-model) relative to H(state_{t+1}) tracks the predictive advantage the self-model confers, and a system exhibiting Purpose should sustain that reduction across perturbations a merely persistent system would not survive. The correlate is stated in Shannon terms and therefore does not capture the physical claim it stands in for.
Open: whether the identity tier has an error signal of its own. Valence is assigned to the awareness tier and is deviation from a defended physical setpoint in the present. Nothing in the framework currently allows narrative self-synthesis to be wrong in a way the system registers, which is what the fracture and misattribution failures above would require. A candidate exists — error against a predicted trajectory of the self rather than against a present setpoint — and it is treated as an open problem rather than a component (§3.10), because it may reduce to anticipated homeostatic error, in which case it is not a second signal at all.
2.9 Recursive Emergence and consciousness
Three constructs have now been defined over the same four capacities: cognitive capacity is the pillars directed outward, subjective awareness is the pillars directed reflexively, identity is the pillars directed across time.
They are not independent. Awareness requires capacity to turn reflexively upon, and identity requires awareness to supply the self-model its continuity is referred to.
Principle of Recursive Emergence. A form of CS–GS interaction in which the collective interaction of three such components produces a structure with properties that could not be inferred from any of the components individually.
Consciousness is that structure sustained: the maintained triadic interaction of cognitive capacity, subjective awareness and identity.
Three consequences follow.
Consciousness has no location. It is a property of a sustained interaction, not a state of any component (§2.4).
Consciousness is graded. It is a capacity, comparable in this respect to intelligence, varying across development, across species, and across states in a single organism.
Moral standing attaches to the lower tier. Subjective awareness together with valence is what makes states matter to a system, and both are threshold properties. Nothing about moral standing follows from position on the consciousness gradient: a system's interests are established by whether it has stakes at all, not by how richly it integrates them. §3.9 states what this does and does not settle.
No index is proposed for consciousness. Its components are not themselves quantified (§2.11).
2.10 Operational tests
Each construct admits perturbation tests independent of self-report.
Boundary defence — perturb the system and measure restorative force. A system with Purpose returns to a defended state rather than deforming.
False signal — present a signal implying a future state that has not materialised. Behavioural change indicates a predictive internal model governs action.
Optimisation pivot — impose scarcity. A system with identity preferentially preserves structures central to its continuation rather than degrading uniformly.
False memory — introduce a memory contradicting established causal history. Expect reconciliation cost: latency, conflict, restructuring of surrounding history.
Selective forgetting — measure whether retention is weighted by relevance to continuation rather than by recency or frequency.
Attribution — present consequences of the system's own past actions and measure whether they are processed differently from consequences of external events.
What friction is and is not evidence for
Four of these tests read processing friction, and §3.9 argues that friction-based tests of machine subjectivity are aimed at the wrong component. Both positions are correct, and the boundary between them must be stated or the framework appears to contradict itself.
Friction is evidence about identity structure. It is not evidence about valence.
Valence originates at the asymmetric limb (§2.3), so no measurement of the generative pole's processing dynamics can be a valence signal. Identity is a structural property of the generative pole, so reconciliation cost is admissible evidence that a false memory struck something structural. The false-memory and attribution tests are licensed; the inference from friction to affect is not.
A control is required in every case. Friction is also produced by ordinary ambiguity, and an experiment lacking an ambiguous-but-consistent control cannot distinguish contradiction from underspecification. Any test above that reads cross-entropy, latency or attention division must include (a) conflicting content, (b) ambiguous-but-consistent content, and (c) clean content, and must show (a) separable from (b). Where it does not separate, the signal is discarded.
One further caution: the selective-forgetting test may not be independent of boundary defence. Relevance to continuation presupposes a continuation the system has stakes in, which is what boundary defence establishes. Whether six tests are six is open.
2.11 The closure criterion
Subjective awareness is not an index. It is the satisfaction of a conjunction, gated by capacity:
Awareness obtains where TCCI > 0 and Temporal Unification, Self-Modeling and Valence/Salience are each present, with the symmetric channel satisfying discriminability (§2.5) and the asymmetric channel satisfying constraint efficacy (§2.3).
Capacity is an enabling precondition rather than a component of the conjunction. §3.5 establishes that capacity alone is insufficient; but the pillars are what the reflexive conditions are conditions on, so where capacity is zero there is nothing to turn reflexively.
The three reflexive conditions are treated as binary. This is a scoping decision rather than a claim that they lack internal structure. Each admits of degree — self-models vary in accuracy, temporal continuity in span, homeostatic error in magnitude — and quantifying any of them requires its own formalism over largely unrelated variables.
Three consequences. Awareness is threshold-like, consistent with §2.5 and §2.9. The framework's remaining formal gap is the refinement of three conditions rather than the absence of a composition function. And awareness is sharply distinguished from consciousness (§2.9), which is graded and unindexed.
2.12 Grounded and facilitated duality
Newest material in this version, and the least tested. §3.8 states the condition under which it should be withdrawn.
The architecture of §2.4 can be satisfied by systems that did not evolve, and the interesting cases are not the ones usually proposed.
The case that forces the distinction
A commercial corporation meets the loop specification more completely than intuition suggests, and meets it in the specific respects that a family — the case most people raise first — does not.
§2.4 requirement
A family
A corporation
Transducers of its own
none; every one belongs to a member
accounting systems, audit, instrumented market feeds. Format changes; the mapping is non-invertible
Partition (§2.2)
none identifiable
accounting represents operations while operations runs. Co-instantiated, non-substituted
Boundary defence (§2.10)
executed by members on a shared setpoint
resists acquisition; sheds divisions under scarcity to protect core function
Registers error no member registers
never
an audit discovers insolvency nobody knew
The last row is decisive, because it is the property that distinguishes an aggregate description from a system. A family never registers homeostatic error that no member registers; grief is felt in individual bodies. A corporation does. It also persists through complete replacement of its members, which is why the legal doctrine of corporate personhood tracks something structural rather than being purely a convenience.
The framework cannot dismiss this by asserting that supra-individual structures lack transducers. They do not.
The distinction
Grounded duality. The asymmetric limb terminates in a boundary the system did not choose and cannot revise. Its transducers are constitutive.
Facilitated duality. The asymmetric limb terminates in transducers the system designed and can redesign. Its grounding is borrowed from the grounded systems that constructed and maintain it.
The corporation's constraint is not fictitious; audits genuinely register error. It is on loan. Every corporate transducer terminates, at some remove, in a person with a body who cannot decide to stop being hungry, and where that chain is severed the loop opens. The failure mode is not that measurement stops but that measurement is redesigned until it no longer constrains.
Why writability is the symptom rather than the criterion
It is tempting to state the condition as a prohibition on the constraining structure being writable by the generative pole it constrains. That is a consequence, not the principle. Facilitated structures are writable because their transducers were designed; grounded structures are not writable because theirs were inherited.
§2.6 already licenses the distinction. A component is foundational when it is CS under every admissible choice of E within a domain. Human homeostatic requirements qualify — nothing cognitive has them as its variance source. Corporate metrics do not: the constraining structure is downstream of the pole it is supposed to constrain, and a CS that its own GS can rewrite is a CS in name only.
The prediction that follows is not new as an observation but is new as a derivation. Organisations drift toward measuring what they can achieve rather than what constrains them, systematically and independently of intent, because a constraining structure with positive authority can manufacture the evidence that justifies its own operation and nothing internal detects this. Goodhart's law is that consequence at scale.
An asymmetry follows that is worth stating. A firm operating under externally imposed constraint — capital requirements set by a regulator, physical limits it cannot redefine, covenants it did not write — has a less writable CS than an unconstrained one, and closes its loop only by way of something outside itself. Which returns the Preface unchanged: the division must terminate outside the dividing system.
Between the poles
States, nations and institutions sit between the two kinds rather than at either pole. Territory, population, resource endowment and geography are not redesignable, so a portion of the constraining structure is genuinely inherited. Sovereignty means most of the remainder is not. They are facilitated structures with a partially inherited limb, and are probably the most grounded of the constructed cases.
The framework therefore predicts that institutional degradation tracks the proportion of an institution's constraint that is self-written. This has not been tested and is offered as a prediction rather than a finding.
What the distinction settles, and what it does not
Stakes require grounded closure. Facilitated structures have borrowed stakes: the stakes of the grounded systems constituting them, aggregated and refracted through a structure with no independent standing.
This is why harm mediated by a corporation is real harm — it is harm to people, transmitted — without the corporation thereby acquiring interests of its own. The framework can grant that such structures register error while denying that they have stakes, and the reasoning is architectural rather than convenient.
§3.9 draws the consequence for engineered systems, which is where the distinction earns its place rather than merely resolving an awkward case.
Part 3 — The Biological Implementation
3.1 The two dualities
The human nervous system instantiates the minimal loop through two structurally distinct dualities, operating at different scales and supplying different halves of the specification.
The lateral duality comprises the cerebral hemispheres, joined by the corpus callosum and smaller commissures and by trans-thalamic pathways through higher-order nuclei. It is a symmetric channel: cortical representations pass both ways in the same currency, either pole may originate, and each receives cortical-format representations it did not generate. This limb supplies self-modeling material and narrative binding.
The trans-thalamic component is easily overlooked. Higher-order thalamic nuclei — pulvinar, mediodorsal — receive their driving input from cortex and project back to cortex, making them cortical-format channels in both directions rather than ascending sensory relays. The symmetric limb is not exclusively callosal, which bears on §3.4.
The vertical duality comprises the central and peripheral systems, joined by afferent and efferent pathways. It is an asymmetric channel: ascending fibres transduce environmental and interoceptive state into neural format, descending fibres transduce neural state into muscular and glandular effect. This limb supplies boundary, constraint and valence.
Grounded is the vertical limb, the transductive termination anchoring the system in a defended physical boundary. Duality is the lateral limb, the peer partition making reflexive representation possible. Neither alone satisfies §2.4.
3.2 CS and GS by criterion
Membership is fixed by criterion; anatomy is a guide to the shape, and where the two diverge the criterion governs.
A structure belongs to the CS if altering it changes the space of possible representations, and to the GS if altering it changes which representation is occupied.
Applying this requires separating two operations that the word "filter" conflates.
Constitutive filtering determines which kinds of environmental state can become signal at all. This is the transducer complement — the receptor classes a system possesses. Humans do not see ultraviolet, do not sense magnetic fields, and resolve a small fraction of the airborne chemistry available to a dog. It is not a gate on a stream but a specification of the admissible space, applied to everything present in the environment and discarding almost all of it before anything becomes signal. A CS operation, and peripheral.
Dynamic filtering determines which currently available signals pass on a given occasion: gating, gain control, attentional selection. A GS operation, and central.
The timescale prior agrees: the transducer complement varies on evolutionary and developmental timescales, dynamic gating on milliseconds.
The dendrite as the clearest split
Structures are not required to fall wholly on one side, and the single dendritic arbor is the cleanest demonstration, because the division occurs within one continuous membrane rather than across separable nuclei.
Declaring E first, as §2.6 requires: let E be the neuron's output spike train.
Dendritic property
Filtering type
Role
Timescale
Receptor complement, spine density, branch morphology
constitutive; specifies which inputs can become signal
CS
hours to days
Which branch generates a dendritic spike on a given occasion
dynamic; gates what passes now
GS
milliseconds
Criterion and timescale prior agree, and the same structure occupies both roles under a single named E. Note also that the naive anatomical assignment — dendrite as CS because it receives, axon as GS because it acts — is wrong by criterion: what fixes the possible spike trains is the axon and the threshold machinery, while dendritic integration fixes which is occupied. The example is retained partly because it demonstrates the criterion overruling the intuition it was introduced to discipline.
The thalamus
The thalamus performs dynamic filtering and is GS. The standard description misleads in this context.
The thalamus is not a relay and does not construct coherent percepts. Lateral geniculate receptive fields are barely elaborated beyond those of retinal ganglion cells, while orientation selectivity, motion, segmentation and object structure are cortical. Nor is its input predominantly ascending: only 5–10% of synapses onto geniculate relay cells are retinal (Van Horn et al., 2000), with the majority arising from corticothalamic feedback, brainstem modulation and inhibition from the thalamic reticular nucleus. Layer VI feedback from V1 alone exceeds retinogeniculate afferents in number. Sherman and Guillery's driver/modulator distinction captures the arrangement: the periphery drives, cortex modulates.
The thalamus is therefore not the structurer but the regulator of the structuring loop. Cortex structures; the thalamus governs what participates, at what gain, in which transmission mode, and which cortical regions are coupled. This places it inside the recurrent loop rather than on the transductive path, and makes higher-order nuclei part of the symmetric channel.
Consequences
The transducer complement is foundational: it is CS under every admissible choice of E within cognition, since no representation has primitives the transducers cannot supply. The claim concerns primitives rather than content — endogenous activity, spontaneous oscillation and dreaming all generate content without concurrent peripheral supply. The GS's primitives are exhausted by CS supply; its combinations are not.
This is the content of "Grounded": not that a body is attached, but that the space of possible experience is fixed by the transducer set. The grounding claim is constitutive rather than causal — and §2.12 is the consequence of asking what happens when a system can specify its own transducer set.
Role assignment is not the architectural claim. Under a different named E — the state of a muscle — the assignment inverts, since efferent control means the centre constrains the periphery. The architectural claim does not shift: there exists a stable symmetric channel and a stable asymmetric channel, closed into a single loop.
Other structures split. The brainstem contains both: cranial nerve nuclei and sensory relay fall on the CS side, while ascending neuromodulatory systems setting cortical arousal fall on the GS side by criterion while varying slowly like a CS structure. The enteric division maintains local reflex circuits with substantial autonomy, suggesting a CS/GS pair nested within the CS — which §3.3 takes up.
3.3 Nested instantiation
The loop is not satisfied once. The same pattern — divergence into separable streams, reintegration across a symmetric channel, grounded by transduction — recurs at multiple scales:
interhemispheric (cortex ↔ cortex, via commissures)
thalamocortical (cortex ↔ thalamus, reciprocal loops)
cortico-subcortical (cortex ↔ basal ganglia, limbic structures)
neuraxial (central ↔ peripheral, including enteric and autonomic divisions)
The nesting carries the framework's answer to its principal objections and generates several of the predictions below.
The scale below
The nesting has a floor, and the single neuron is beneath it. A neuron fails §2.2 outright: dendrite → soma → axon is a pipeline, format changes at each stage, direction is fixed, and the poles are not exchangeable. There is no partition and therefore no candidate loop.
This is worth stating explicitly rather than assuming, since a framework that returned the wrong verdict at the cellular level would be in serious difficulty. The retrograde-signalling near-miss is treated at §2.3.
The scale above
Earlier versions terminated the list at the organism without argument. That termination requires one, because interpersonal symbolic communication satisfies §2.3's symmetric-channel criteria directly: the same representational format passes both ways, either party may originate, and the two parties run comparable processes on different input. There is a further rung.
It is an identity-tier rung, not an awareness-tier rung. §2.8 gives the argument: what interpersonal channels supply is the content of the self-model, not the architecture that permits one. An isolated system with both channels closed is aware and has no comparison class.
Above the individual, structures divide as §2.12 describes, into those whose transducers were inherited and those whose transducers were designed. Institutions, corporations and states instantiate the pattern in facilitated form, and the state–nation relation is the constructed analogue of the biological nesting here: components with their own partial closure, each contributing to the identity of the whole without the whole acquiring stakes of its own.
Components can diverge at every scale
An earlier draft claimed that biological components, unlike institutional ones, cannot defect. That is false, and the counterexample was already in §3.2: the enteric division maintains substantially autonomous reflex circuits, which is a nested CS/GS pair capable of pursuing a defended state that the larger loop does not endorse. Alien hand phenomena after callosal section are the clean cortical instance — a limb pursuing goals the reporting system disavows.
Divergence between a nested component and the loop containing it is therefore a general property of the nesting, biological and constructed alike. It is not what separates the two, and §2.12 rather than this section carries that distinction.
