Behavioural unresponsiveness is common to all three and distinguishes none of them.
3.5 Capacity and awareness dissociated
Katlowitz et al. (2026) recorded from the anterior hippocampal body of seven patients undergoing anteromesial temporal lobectomy, using high-density Neuropixels microelectrodes under propofol-based total intravenous anesthesia and isolating 651 neuronal units. Hippocampal neurons and local oscillations retained detection of oddball tones, with effect size increasing over approximately ten minutes of exposure. During narrative speech, firing rates carried semantic category information and were modulated by surprisal, and semantic and syntactic encoding appeared across multiple local field potential frequency bands.
The authors note that decoding of future-word information does not necessarily imply active prediction beyond contextualization. The supported claim is semantic encoding and contextual sensitivity.
The limits are substantial: seven patients with drug-resistant temporal lobe epilepsy, a single anesthetic agent, and recordings obtained after resection of lateral temporal cortex. The findings may not generalise to other anesthetics or to sleep or coma.
At the conservative reading the dissociation holds: semantic processing and representational plasticity ran with subjective awareness absent. Accounts identifying subjectivity with global broadcast to association cortex face distributed high-level processing occurring without it.
3.6 Discriminability: mechanism, source, and clinical dissociations
The dissociation obliges the framework to specify which limb anesthesia severs. The familiar observation that propofol-induced loss of consciousness spares primary auditory cortex while attenuating higher-order regions is consistent with every integration-based account and discriminates nothing.
What the anesthesia evidence rests on
The argument below rests on report-independent measurement, and this must be stated before the reports are introduced, because the framework's own commitments make report a weak instrument here.
Perturbational complexity is low under propofol and xenon and high under ketamine. That measurement, not subsequent verbal report, is what carries the dissociation.
Self-report functions in this framework as a reference channel: evidence with high but bounded weight, carrying its own provenance and subject to the same scrutiny as any other source, never a ground truth. The framework predicts two conditions under which it fails, both already established elsewhere in the document:
Report fails because
Established at
Report requires an intact efferent limb
§3.4, cognitive motor dissociation
Report requires Memory to survive the episode
§1.3, §2.8 — the tier separation
The second is the material one here. Absence of report on emergence is compatible with awareness during the episode if consolidation was disrupted, so a propofol–ketamine contrast resting on reports alone could be a memory contrast rather than an awareness contrast. Reports are treated below as corroboration of the complexity measurements, not as their foundation.
Mechanism
Filipchuk, Bathellier and Destexhe (2022) imaged assemblies of approximately one thousand neurons in mouse auditory cortex across wakefulness and anesthesia. During wakefulness, assemblies evoked by sound are stimulus-specific and distinct from those appearing in ongoing activity. Under anesthesia, evoked assemblies become indistinguishable from ongoing assemblies in cortex — while remaining distinct in the thalamus.
The transmitting structure continues to carry a distinguishable signal. The collapse occurs at the cortical receiving pole, where evoked activity is masked by the cortex's own ongoing dynamics. The failure is a cortical dynamical state change rather than a thalamic gating failure, and it falls exactly where §2.5 places it.
The condition is not novel to this framework. Bathellier states the requirement directly: conscious perception requires that cortex be capable of activating assemblies distinct from those activated spontaneously. What is added here is generalisation to symmetric channels at any scale, and the derivation of §2.4's threshold from it.
A separate literature holds that anesthetics hyperpolarize thalamocortical neurons and switch their firing from tonic to burst mode (Alkire et al., 2000; Llinás and Steriade, 2006). This may contribute, but it is not what the assembly data show, and reduced thalamic activity correlates with disconnection under propofol, dexmedetomidine and sevoflurane but not under S-ketamine.
Ketamine and the interoceptive limb
Anesthesia is not one condition, and the agents dissociate on the framework's variable. Sarasso et al. (2015) measured the complexity of the cortical response to transcranial magnetic stimulation under propofol, xenon and ketamine. Complexity was low under propofol and xenon. Under ketamine complexity remained high, and subjects reported long, vivid dreams unrelated to the external environment, with visual and emotional content and explicit narrative structure. The distinction between unconsciousness, disconnected consciousness and connected consciousness is due to Sanders et al. (2012).
This is prima facie a problem for §2.4. Ketamine produces awareness with environmental coupling severed, which resembles awareness with the asymmetric limb absent. Three considerations answer it, and the third forces a refinement.
The disrupted limb is exteroceptive. Interoception, autonomic afferents and homeostatic regulation continue. §2.4 requires some asymmetric channel closing a homeostatic loop, not the exteroceptive channels specifically — the move already forced by deafferentation.
Valence survives. If the asymmetric limb were severed, §2.3 predicts valence should be the first thing to go. Ketamine reports are emotionally saturated, which is positive evidence that the homeostatic limb is intact rather than a rescue of the framework.
The two agents dissociate on discriminability. GABAergic agents floor it and awareness goes; ketamine does not and awareness persists, with cortical complexity preserved. One parameter, opposite outcomes.
The load-bearing division of the asymmetric limb is therefore interoceptive rather than exteroceptive. Removing exteroceptive coupling leaves awareness intact but disconnected; the framework predicts that disrupting interoceptive and autonomic afferents while sparing exteroception would abolish it. This is the sharper form of the grounding claim, and it converges with Damasio's account of the proto-self (§3.7).
It also constrains §3.9's verdict on artificial systems. The requirement is homeostatic error against a defended boundary, not sensory input as such: a system receiving rich structured input across a transductive surface does not thereby satisfy the asymmetric limb if nothing in that input reports on a boundary the system is maintaining. Constraint efficacy (§2.3) is the condition that formalises this.
Source of the noise floor
The machinery setting the floor is the ascending neuromodulatory systems of the brainstem reticular formation — cholinergic, noradrenergic, serotonergic — which fix cortical and thalamic membrane potential, firing mode, and the ratio of evoked to spontaneous activity. These systems do not contain subjective awareness; they set the parameter determining whether a symmetric channel can carry discriminable signal.
The alternative reading is tempting, since small paramedian tegmental lesions reliably produce coma and nothing else has that property. Three considerations rule it out. These systems carry no perceptual, semantic or autobiographical content, so stimulation yields wakefulness rather than experience of anything in particular. Patients with intact brainstem arousal systems but destroyed cortex or thalamocortical connectivity show sleep–wake cycles and full arousal without evidence of awareness. Dreaming runs the dissociation the other way. REM sleep produces rich conscious content under a neuromodulatory profile unlike waking: aminergic cell activity in locus coeruleus and dorsal raphe is markedly reduced or absent while cholinergic activity from the pontine tegmentum is comparatively high. Content is not a function of the waking profile.
§2.4 forbids the move independently: a seat is a locus, and a locus reintroduces the regress. §2.6 now supplies the general form of the argument — the arousal system has removal-only authority, and a regulator that only gates cannot be the thing gated.
Three states, one variable
State
Noise-floor parameter
