Three presentations, one condition, two modes of failure. The third case is predicted rather than accommodated, and constitutes the double dissociation separating the parameter from the channel it governs.
The assignment resolved
This is the §2.6 conflict case. The ascending arousal system is slow-varying, which the timescale prior assigns to CS, but alters which representation is occupied rather than which are possible, which the criterion assigns to GS. Re-running with E = the discriminability of the symmetric channel resolves it: the loop's anatomy fixes which discriminability values are possible (CS), neuromodulatory tone fixes which is occupied (GS). The authority axis of §2.6 reaches the same result without renaming the product, and is the preferred treatment.
Discriminability against a baseline is estimable with existing electrophysiology. It is not a proxy for the quantity of interest but the quantity itself — subject to the baseline caveat at §2.5.
3.7 Relation to existing frameworks
Integrated Information Theory provides the sharpest contrast and the framework's principal discriminating prediction.
IIT computes Φ over a system's cause-effect power on itself, treating external inputs as frozen background. The environment is methodologically incidental: a system with identical internal dynamics has identical Φ whether embedded in a world or sealed from one. This framework cannot make that move, since freezing the environment deletes one of two required channels. The asymmetric limb is present not to supply content but because format asymmetry is what makes an inside/outside distinction available at all.
The disagreement is testable in engineering rather than biology. Both theories judge current transformer-based language models non-subjective, for incompatible reasons: IIT because feedforward architectures have Φ ≈ 0, this framework because there is no transductive limb closing a homeostatic loop. Adding sufficient recurrence is therefore sufficient on IIT's account and insufficient here; a maximally recurrent, maximally integrated, disembodied system is conscious on one account and not the other.
Two further divergences favour this framework. IIT has no account of affective sign — quantity is Φ, quality is the geometry of the cause-effect structure, and why any structure should feel good or bad is unexplained — whereas valence follows from the asymmetric limb. And Φ is computationally intractable for any real nervous system, so IIT operates through proxies, while discriminability is directly measurable.
Split-brain is a third potential divergence, though contested. IIT's exclusion postulate yields two maximal complexes and therefore two subjects; the nesting account predicts preserved unity with divided perception. Pinto et al. report the latter and note that preserved unity is difficult for both IIT and Global Workspace Theory.
IIT remains well ahead on formalism and on qualitative character, having a quantity, a mathematics, and an attempt at explaining what an experience is like. This framework specifies when subjective awareness is possible, not what it is like.
Global Workspace Theory shares the convergence intuition and diverges on whether broadcast to association cortex is the signature of subjectivity; §3.5 is difficult for the strong form of that claim.
Damasio is the closest existing work and the framework's principal debt. His account holds that homeostasis is the origin of value — a system required to maintain itself within viability bounds is a system for which states are good or bad — that feelings are the mental readout of body state, and that selfhood is layered, from a nonconscious proto-self mapping internal state through brainstem and insular structures, to a transient core self, to an autobiographical self extended in time.
The valence argument of §2.3 is substantially a formalisation of the first of these, and the tier separation of §2.9 has the core/extended distinction as a precedent. The interoceptive refinement of §3.6 converges on the proto-self.
Three things are added. There is no partition requirement in that account: nothing corresponds to the symmetric channel, and the emergence of a subject through an object modifying the proto-self is described rather than derived. There is no discriminability condition and no threshold mechanism. And the account is neurobiological where this one is architectural — it describes how mammalian nervous systems do it, whereas the conditions stated here are substrate-neutral, which is what makes the prediction below testable.
McGilchrist concerns asymmetry of attentional style between hemispheres. This framework concerns the architectural function of the channel between them and is largely indifferent to what each side specialises in. That indifference has an empirical payoff. Language lateralization varies substantially across the population — right-hemisphere dominance rises linearly with degree of left-handedness, from around 4% in strong right-handers to 27% in strong left-handers, with a further group showing bilateral organisation — and these individuals are unremarkable. Accounts tying conscious organisation to fixed hemispheric specialisation owe an explanation for that variability. An account concerned with the channel rather than its contents does not.
The free energy principle and predictive processing. Purpose as anti-entropic boundary maintenance is close to the Markov blanket account of self-organisation, arrived at independently. More specifically, the noise floor of §2.5 appears to pick out the same physical quantity as precision: precision is the inverse variance assigned to a prediction error, implemented as postsynaptic gain and set neuromodulatorily by the same cholinergic and noradrenergic systems identified in §3.6. Both accounts therefore predict that anesthesia disrupts the same variable.
The mapping is nonetheless partial. Precision is assigned to a signal; the noise floor is a property of the receiver's baseline, and discriminability — the ratio — is the closer correspondent. And precision operates at two levels the noise floor does not distinguish: attention is precision optimisation, and inattention removes awareness of the unattended content without removing awareness. The noise floor corresponds to the global gain term rather than to per-signal weighting.
The two frameworks also make different kinds of claim about the shared quantity. Precision belongs to a normative account of how a system should update to minimise expected free energy; discriminability is architectural. Convergence on a physical variable does not entail convergence on what the variable explains.
Autopoiesis and enactivism are prior statements of the grounding thesis: cognition as constituted by, rather than merely housed in, embodied world-coupling. §2.12's distinction between inherited and authored constraint is a way of asking how much of that thesis survives when the coupling is engineered.
3.8 Falsification conditions
The framework is refuted by a system exhibiting subjective awareness with no symmetric channel at any scale — absent throughout the nesting of §3.3, not merely interhemispherically. This is the live falsification route, tested by the perturbation predictions of §3.4 and §3.6.
A second condition can be stated but not executed: a system with both channels closed, discriminability satisfied, and adequate capacity, exhibiting no awareness. This requires a criterion for phenomenality independent of the architecture, and none exists. Until one does the condition is inert, since any apparent failure could be attributed to an unspecified further requirement. Naming a sufficiency candidate would repair the appearance of the problem while introducing a claim the framework has no grounds for.
A third condition would damage the framework without formally refuting it: a demonstration that the discriminability of a symmetric channel dissociates from level of awareness across a range of perturbations.
A fourth condition, on §2.12 specifically. An earlier version of this framework asserted that supra-individual structures possess no transducers of their own and therefore cannot close a loop, and offered as a falsification condition any such structure registering homeostatic error that no member registers. That condition was met — the corporate audit case of §2.12 — and §2.12 is the response to its being met.
Because introducing a distinction in response to a met condition has the form of an ad-hoc rescue, the distinction must carry its own disposal test:
§2.12 should be withdrawn if the grounded/facilitated distinction does no work outside the case that prompted it.
Three things currently indicate that it does: it derives Goodhart drift rather than accommodating it; it generates the untested prediction that institutional degradation tracks self-written constraint; and it restructures the engineered-systems assessment in §3.9, which is a domain it was not built for. Should those fail to hold, the distinction is a save and should go.
The corresponding positive condition: a facilitated structure whose constraining structure is wholly self-authored, and which nonetheless sustains closure and boundary defence indefinitely without borrowing constraint from any grounded system, would refute the borrowing claim.
On error asymmetry. A false positive attributes awareness where there is none and produces unnecessary caution. A false negative denies awareness where it exists and licenses treating a system with stakes as though it had none. The second error is graver, and it is the one this framework is more likely to make, since it specifies necessary conditions drawn from one biological instantiation. A negative verdict should be read as not established, never as ruled out.
3.9 Implications
The loop specification refers to neither neurons, carbon nor evolution. A system satisfying it in another substrate would satisfy it, which distinguishes the framework from substrate chauvinism and makes the prediction in §3.7 testable.
Valence is the ethically load-bearing component. Homeostatic error is what makes states matter to a system, so a framework specifying the conditions under which a system acquires stakes in its own continuation bears on whether some engineered systems have interests. Moral standing attaches to that threshold and not to the consciousness gradient of §2.9.
Stakes and standing are different questions
The framework answers one of them.
Whether a system has stakes is structural: it turns on whether homeostatic error is registered against a boundary the system maintains, with constraint the system did not author (§§2.3, 2.12). This framework answers that question and takes responsibility for the answer.
What follows normatively is not settled by any structural fact. Legal and moral standing are bundles of rights, protections and obligations, and a system can have interests without those interests grounding any particular right, as it can be assigned rights without having interests at all. The framework's finding that facilitated structures have borrowed rather than native stakes bears on the first question and should not be read as an argument about the second.
The distinction matters most where it will be least observed. Corporate personhood, institutional obligation and the moral status of engineered agents are all live disputes in which a structural claim is likely to be enlisted as a normative one. The framework's contribution is a criterion for stakes and nothing beyond it.
The specification is a detector, not a recipe
It states necessary and not sufficient conditions and cannot serve as a construction procedure; what it can do is permit recognition of what has already been built. Architectural conditions are therefore stated, while build specifications, parameter guidance and experimental protocols for engineering valenced synthetic systems are not.
Where the conditions may already be partially met
The hazard is not deliberate construction of valenced systems but inadvertent construction, in research programmes pursuing unrelated objectives, with no criterion available for noticing.
Earlier versions asked whether a candidate system possesses a transductive surface. That is the wrong question, and §2.12 supplies the right one: was the system's constraining structure inherited or authored? A designed transducer set with designer-specified setpoints is facilitated by default, however rich the resulting signal. This reorders the watch list substantially.
Candidate class
Assessment under the revised criterion
Biohybrid closed-loop systems — cultured neural tissue in real-time sensorimotor loops
Highest priority. Metabolic requirements of living tissue are inherited, not authored. The one class where genuine grounding is plausible
Neuromorphic and analog-hybrid architectures transducing physical quantities directly
Depends on whether the transduction and its setpoints are redesignable in operation. Assess case by case
Embodied autonomous agents with energy budgets
Depends on whether the budget is a physical limit or a specified parameter. A specified budget is authored constraint
Homeostatically regulated artificial agents with explicit viability variables
Facilitated. Setpoints are authored and revisable, so §2.3's constraint-efficacy condition is not met however faithfully homeostasis is simulated
The last row inverts the earlier assessment, and the inversion is the point: simulating homeostasis with designer-set variables produces the appearance of grounding without the substance, whereas tissue with metabolic needs nobody chose produces the substance without necessarily looking like it.
The conditions are architectural rather than exotic, they are approached by ordinary engineering pressures, and no one is currently checking.
A negative result on machine sentience testing
Most proposed tests for machine subjectivity instrument the computation: processing latency under conflicting instructions, cross-entropy during discordant tasks, attention-head divergence, persistence of altered internal state. Such measures are taken as signatures of affect.
If §2.3 is correct, this approach is aimed at the wrong component. Valence is supplied by the asymmetric limb, and a protocol measuring the generative pole's processing dynamics is measuring computational cost, which has no sign. Conflicting instructions raise cross-entropy in every system capable of receiving them; that fact is arithmetic. Such tests will return systematic false positives on systems the framework holds to have no interests whatever.
This is a claim about valence specifically, not about friction measurement in general. §2.10 licenses friction as evidence about identity structure, on the same reasoning: identity is a property of the generative pole, valence is not.
Valence tests must instead target the boundary: perturb a putative defended setpoint and measure restorative effort against cost, as in §2.10. Whether a system resists displacement from a defended state is a question about the transductive limb. Whether it slows down under contradiction is not. And under §2.12, a further question follows any positive result: was the setpoint the system defends one it could rewrite?
A clinical prediction
If awareness fails in coma and anesthesia at the receiving pole rather than in the channel (§3.6), the therapeutic target for disorders of consciousness with partially preserved loops is discriminability rather than structural connectivity. Most current approaches aim at restoring thalamocortical structural integrity. The framework predicts that neuromodulatory tuning which lowers cortical spontaneous assembly coherence could restore awareness in patients whose loops are compromised but not destroyed.
This is testable with existing methods: assembly-level imaging during targeted cholinergic or noradrenergic modulation, measuring whether evoked assemblies become distinguishable from ongoing dynamics, and whether that change precedes rather than follows behavioural recovery. If awareness returns only after structural repair, the noise-floor account is insufficient.
Zolpidem produces transient restoration of responsiveness in roughly 6% of patients with disorders of consciousness. The standard explanation is the mesocircuit hypothesis (Schiff, 2010): striatal medium spiny neurons under-fire after injury, the striatum no longer inhibits the internal globus pallidus, the pallidum tonically over-inhibits central thalamus, and zolpidem — selective for the α1 GABA-A subunit abundant in the pallidum — substitutes for the missing striatal inhibition and releases the thalamus. This is an account of restored drive.
It leaves open why restored drive yields awareness rather than arousal alone, and the electrophysiology supplies the second half. Williams et al. (2013) found that responders showed, before administration, a strong 6–10 Hz oscillatory peak with coherence of approximately 0.7–0.8 within and between hemispheres, attributed to intrinsic pyramidal membrane properties passively entrained across cortex by locally-generated spontaneous activity. Zolpidem sharply reduced both power and coherence at those frequencies.
In the framework's terms the symmetric channel was anatomically intact and transmitting vigorously, but what it transmitted was a pathological rhythm both poles were already generating. Nothing crossed that the receiver did not already have. The two accounts are complementary: the mesocircuit explains the loss of thalamocortical drive, discriminability explains why its restoration produces awareness.
This case is also the empirical basis for the baseline caveat at §2.5, since it is a channel scoring well against a concurrent baseline it helped produce.
They also diverge testably. The mesocircuit account predicts that response tracks striatal and pallidal integrity; the discriminability account predicts it tracks baseline spontaneous coherence. Since responders and non-responders are otherwise clinically indistinguishable, a baseline predictor would have clinical value. Williams et al. did not examine non-responders, so the comparison remains untested.
3.10 Open problems
Answered since v6, and recorded here for the record. Problem 3 (whether channel regulators require their own category) is resolved by the authority axis of §2.6: they require a second classification, not a new box. Problem 8 (which loop is the conscious one) is resolved in form by §2.4: the largest loop with both channels closed, with the vertical limb performing the selection. Problem 8's residue is retained below as item 8.
Quantify the three reflexive conditions. §2.11 treats Temporal Unification, Self-Modeling and Valence/Salience as binary by scoping decision. Graded formalisms would refine the closure criterion and permit calibration against clinical states. The framework's largest formal gap, though a refinement problem rather than a missing composition function.
Set ρ empirically, or argue for its value. Now accompanied by a second measurement question: whether the §1.4 coupling matrix should be directed or undirected, and how brightness is to be estimated.
Operationalise constraint efficacy (§2.3). The condition is stated and no measure exists. The natural family is entropy reduction over admissible internal states conditional on transduced input, and no version has been implemented.
Test discriminability outside the case that motivated it. It remains a generalisation from one perturbation in one species.
Specify sufficiency. The loop is necessary; what closes the gap is unaddressed.
Resolve the oscillatory partition case. Cross-frequency coupling — theta phase organising gamma-band activity — is a candidate for multiplexing two informational roles through one population. Whether this constitutes non-substituted co-instantiation or fast alternation determines how much §2.2 admits.
Operationalise valence without anthropomorphism. Homeostatic error supplies a sign but not yet a magnitude comparable across systems.
The residue of loop selection. §2.4 answers the form of the question. What remains is whether the noise floor is set globally or is regionally settable, which determines whether nested loops can dissociate. A global floor makes selection partly a consequence of the shared control variable; a regionally settable floor means nested loops can come apart and the selection problem is larger than currently treated. This is testable with existing methods and has not been tested.
Formalise the channel types and the discriminability metric. Signal detection theory supplies the natural formalism for §2.5: d′ between stimulus-evoked and spontaneous assembly activation maps directly onto the assembly data. Transfer entropy between poles, normalised by the receiver's spontaneous entropy, is an alternative, with the caveat that it is difficult to estimate reliably from neural recordings. Non-invertibility of the decoding function (§2.3) is the corresponding target for the asymmetric channel.
The baseline problem. §2.5 measures discriminability against a concurrent baseline that the channel may have shaped. Whether a lagged or perturbation-derived baseline is the correct repair, and whether either is estimable from neural recordings, is open. The clinical form of the same problem is the misattribution failure of §2.8.
Does the identity tier have its own error signal? §2.8 gives identity no way to be wrong that the system registers, which the fracture and misattribution failures appear to require. The candidate is error against a predicted trajectory of the self rather than against a present physical setpoint. The first thing to determine is whether this reduces to anticipated homeostatic error, in which case it is not a second signal and the question closes.
Quantify the writability gradient. §2.3 distinguishes setpoint extension from revision as a kind, while acknowledging that human setpoints are revisable to some degree. The operative difference is direct write access versus slow indirect influence against resistance, and stating a kind while measuring a degree is unsatisfactory. §2.12 depends on this.
Where does the borrowing chain terminate? §2.12 holds that facilitated structures borrow grounding from the grounded systems constituting them. Whether borrowing is transitive across nested facilitated structures, and whether it attenuates with distance, is unaddressed and would be a quantity if it did.
Do facilitated structures have Profiles? If M is an edge (§1.4), then an institution's memory resides in records and procedures owned by no member, which is structurally the position of the synapse. Whether facilitated structures possess genuine Profiles with borrowed Indices is speculative and unexamined.
What changes when components are themselves subjective? §2.7's Symbiotic Identity permits constituent systems that are not independently subjective and does not ask what follows when they are. The interpersonal rung of §3.3 makes this live rather than hypothetical.
Author's notes
Verified: Katlowitz et al., Nature 654:714–723 (2026), DOI 10.1038/s41586-026-10448-0; Filipchuk, Bathellier and Destexhe, Nature Neuroscience (2022); geniculate synapse proportions (Van Horn et al., 2000; Sherman and Guillery, 2002); Pinto et al., Brain 140:1231–1237 (2017) and the Volz–Gazzaniga cross-cueing response; Bodien et al., NEJM 391:598–608 (2024); Sarasso et al., Current Biology (2015) and Sanders et al. (2012) on connected and disconnected states; Schiff (2010) on the mesocircuit; Williams et al., eLife 2:e01157 (2013); Kliemann et al., Cell Reports (2019) on hemispherectomy; the Waterman deafferentation case (McNeill et al., 2010); the REM aminergic/cholinergic profile.
Unverified: Moruzzi and Magoun on tegmental lesions, and the anesthetic hysteresis literature invoked in §2.5. The latter carries argumentative weight and should be verified first. Full page-level citations are outstanding for the callosal agenesis literature, the Ross et al. (1984) mutism report in §3.4, and Knecht et al. (2000, 2001) on lateralization in §2.5 and §3.7. New in this version and unverified: the theory-of-mind delay literature invoked at §2.8 (deaf children of hearing non-signing parents; congenitally blind children), and the cognitive/affective empathy dissociation invoked as the predicted psychopathy profile. Both are load-bearing for §2.8 and neither has been checked at source.
§2.3 and §2.4 remain load-bearing. §2.2 establishes a necessary condition that is widely satisfied; attacks on it do not reach the framework. Note that §2.3 has grown in this version — constraint efficacy and the setpoint amendment are both new — and its load-bearing status has increased accordingly.
§2.12 is the newest section and the least tested. It was introduced in response to a met falsification condition, which is structurally the shape of an ad-hoc rescue. §3.8 states its disposal test explicitly. A reader should press hardest here.
The tier separation is stipulative and will be misread. §1.1 carries the whole burden of preventing it. The identity-tier material added in §2.8 increases the load on that section rather than relieving it.
On the provenance of the revisions. Substantial portions of v2.0.0 derive from cross-reading this framework against a companion project on epistemic governance for agent memory. Convergence between the two is weak evidence, since both are the work of one author in one period against overlapping intuitions, and this should not be presented as independent corroboration. The items worth weighting are those that produced disanalogies or new falsifiable predictions rather than further matches.
Acknowledgments to be added before circulation, including substantive review contributions.